1
00:00:01,202 --> 00:00:01,903
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, right.

2
00:00:02,263 --> 00:00:13,591
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, today I thought of going through the couple of short suttas that are sort of related, a bit of an overlap in terms of a theme.

3
00:00:14,472 --> 00:00:27,542
[SPEAKER_01]: One is Anguttara 3.113, Apāyaka Sutta, and then the other one is Anguttara 3.112, the one before that, but we'll do it after, which is Dhūtya Nidana Sutta.

4
00:00:30,064 --> 00:00:30,404
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

5
00:00:33,096 --> 00:00:39,538
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'll just read to them first, because they're quite... they're fairly short, and then we can... we can go through the points, things that come up in questions.

6
00:00:40,778 --> 00:00:49,681
[SPEAKER_01]: So, Apāyika Sutta says, because there are these three, who are destined for deprivation, destined for hell, having not abandoned this.

7
00:00:50,301 --> 00:00:50,801
[SPEAKER_01]: What three?

8
00:00:52,102 --> 00:00:55,623
[SPEAKER_01]: One who is not celibate, yet has undertaken the claim of being celibate.

9
00:00:56,443 --> 00:01:00,624
[SPEAKER_01]: One who, without grounds, accuses a person who is celibate of not being celibate.

10
00:01:02,386 --> 00:01:08,068
[SPEAKER_01]: And the one who is such a speaker and holder of such a view, that there is no fault in central pleasures.

11
00:01:08,728 --> 00:01:12,049
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of that view, he falls headfirst into central pleasures.

12
00:01:12,470 --> 00:01:20,853
[SPEAKER_01]: These bhikkhus are the three, three types of people, who are destined for deprivation, destined for hell, having not abandoned this.

13
00:01:27,891 --> 00:01:30,413
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there are these three origins for the arising of action.

14
00:01:30,433 --> 00:01:30,954
[SPEAKER_01]: What three?

15
00:01:31,574 --> 00:01:35,917
[SPEAKER_01]: With regard to past phenomena that are grounds for desire and lust, desire arises.

16
00:01:36,818 --> 00:01:40,901
[SPEAKER_01]: With regard to future phenomena that are grounds for desire and lust, desire arises.

17
00:01:41,742 --> 00:01:45,585
[SPEAKER_01]: With regard to present phenomena that are grounds for desire and lust, desire arises.

18
00:01:47,286 --> 00:01:52,911
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how big does desire arise with regard to past, future, present phenomena that are grounds for desire and lust?

19
00:01:53,772 --> 00:02:01,497
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, with reference to past, future, present phenomena, they're the grounds for desire and lust, one repeatedly thinks and ponders them along the same lines with the mind.

20
00:02:04,699 --> 00:02:10,022
[SPEAKER_01]: While one is repeatedly thinking and pondering along the same lines with their mind, desire arises.

21
00:02:10,423 --> 00:02:18,488
[SPEAKER_01]: When desire has arisen, one becomes fettered to those phenomena, and this we quite call a fetter, which is the lust of the mind, the mind's lust.

22
00:02:20,690 --> 00:02:26,132
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus bhikkhu, desire arises with reference to the past, future, present phenomena, the other grounds, for desire and lust to arise.

23
00:02:29,113 --> 00:02:35,835
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then Buddha goes and explains the opposite, saying, bhikkhus, there are these three origins for the non-arising of action.

24
00:02:36,235 --> 00:02:36,735
[SPEAKER_01]: What three?

25
00:02:37,455 --> 00:02:42,017
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, with regard to the past phenomena, the other grounds for desire and lust, desire does not arise.

26
00:02:43,390 --> 00:02:46,391
[SPEAKER_01]: With regard to the future phenomena, data grounds for desire and lust, desire does not arise.

27
00:02:46,411 --> 00:02:49,372
[SPEAKER_01]: With regard to the present phenomena, data grounds for desire and lust, desire does not arise.

28
00:02:50,032 --> 00:02:54,573
[SPEAKER_01]: The point being, removal of desire equals non-action.

29
00:02:54,673 --> 00:02:56,434
[SPEAKER_01]: Removal of kāma, removal of action.

30
00:02:57,094 --> 00:02:59,215
[SPEAKER_01]: We'll see that later, obviously, get into more detail.

31
00:03:00,095 --> 00:03:06,077
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how because does desire not arise with regard to the past, future, present phenomena, data grounds for desire and lust?

32
00:03:07,030 --> 00:03:11,852
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, one understands the subsequent results of past, future, present phenomena that are grounds for desire and lust.

33
00:03:12,652 --> 00:03:15,553
[SPEAKER_01]: Having known the subsequent results, one turns away from them.

34
00:03:16,154 --> 00:03:20,315
[SPEAKER_01]: Having turned away, having penetrated with the mind, one sees through with wisdom.

35
00:03:21,536 --> 00:03:29,839
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, desire does not arise with regard to past phenomena that are grounds for desire and lust, and that's, Bhikkhu, is the origin for the non-arising of action.

36
00:03:35,058 --> 00:03:35,258
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

37
00:03:36,258 --> 00:04:00,965
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, if we go back to the to the beginning, to the first sutta and the three types of people that are destined for deprivation.

38
00:04:02,185 --> 00:04:04,106
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you could see that what's in common

39
00:04:06,088 --> 00:04:16,911
[SPEAKER_01]: What's in common to these individuals, put it that way, would be like a level of perversion of the truth.

40
00:04:17,371 --> 00:04:18,051
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's put it that way.

41
00:04:18,991 --> 00:04:35,076
[SPEAKER_01]: A certain amount of that kind of existential discrepancy that they maintained, which is what constitutes the weight of depravity, the weight of their karma that will make them, you know, go to hell and so on.

42
00:04:35,976 --> 00:04:44,162
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not just to say like, oh, anybody that engages in this reality is destined for hell, but also, of course, it is pointing in that direction.

43
00:04:44,202 --> 00:04:50,387
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, that's kind of the whole Mara's trap and taming of the senses and everything the Buddha talks about in virtually every other sutta.

44
00:04:52,148 --> 00:05:02,876
[SPEAKER_01]: So, but obviously, in this case, it's like the three kind of obvious and, you know, not even so subtle types of intentional choice

45
00:05:03,838 --> 00:05:06,638
[SPEAKER_01]: That results in perversion of the obvious.

46
00:05:07,759 --> 00:05:11,879
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the degrees of perversion determine the depth of hell.

47
00:05:12,359 --> 00:05:13,120
[SPEAKER_01]: We can put it that way.

48
00:05:14,020 --> 00:05:17,880
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you could see, like, what's in common to these things.

49
00:05:18,040 --> 00:05:22,061
[SPEAKER_01]: As I've asked before, it's kind of...

50
00:05:22,081 --> 00:05:29,222
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have a person... Well, say, you have a person who hears about how sensuality is bad.

51
00:05:30,403 --> 00:05:32,063
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sensuality should at least be

52
00:05:33,453 --> 00:05:36,515
[SPEAKER_01]: Curbed, moderated, kept in check.

53
00:05:36,795 --> 00:05:40,358
[SPEAKER_01]: You shouldn't just take it for granted and blindly engage with it.

54
00:05:41,459 --> 00:05:44,441
[SPEAKER_01]: And then they, you know, maybe want to want to do it even further.

55
00:05:44,461 --> 00:05:45,942
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, no, I really want to...

56
00:05:46,062 --> 00:05:47,803
[SPEAKER_01]: I see the problem of sensual desires.

57
00:05:47,863 --> 00:05:49,304
[SPEAKER_01]: I really want to free myself from it.

58
00:05:49,344 --> 00:05:51,386
[SPEAKER_01]: So they take on the vow of celibacy.

59
00:05:52,026 --> 00:05:56,890
[SPEAKER_01]: To train, to train in in abandoning of those fundamental deeply rooted desires.

60
00:05:58,811 --> 00:05:59,532
[SPEAKER_01]: But then they break it.

61
00:06:00,733 --> 00:06:03,902
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, it's that choice of perversion that comes up.

62
00:06:03,922 --> 00:06:06,990
[SPEAKER_01]: Choice of discrepancy would be, when they break it,

63
00:06:08,874 --> 00:06:11,095
[SPEAKER_01]: They do not confess it.

64
00:06:11,535 --> 00:06:12,876
[SPEAKER_01]: They do not admit it.

65
00:06:13,536 --> 00:06:17,958
[SPEAKER_01]: They do not rec... well, they don't dare to, like, basically acknowledge the false.

66
00:06:17,978 --> 00:06:19,479
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they pretend they're still doing it.

67
00:06:20,299 --> 00:06:28,863
[SPEAKER_01]: And it might not even be that, say, like a person, like, kind of pre-plans that, oh, I will say I'm celibate or not.

68
00:06:28,943 --> 00:06:30,504
[SPEAKER_01]: It might be that they're lying to themselves.

69
00:06:30,724 --> 00:06:34,065
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it might be, like, a subtle kind of bad faith of, oh, yeah, no, I didn't mean to.

70
00:06:34,486 --> 00:06:35,366
[SPEAKER_01]: I am still celibate.

71
00:06:35,566 --> 00:06:37,227
[SPEAKER_01]: And they genuinely believe they're celibate.

72
00:06:38,324 --> 00:06:43,968
[SPEAKER_01]: While, actually, they keep breaking and contradicting that very, very claim, very state of being.

73
00:06:45,329 --> 00:06:52,733
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not just like, oh, I say I'm celibate and I lied, or something, once or twice, and then, you know, I saw the...

74
00:06:52,994 --> 00:06:54,334
[SPEAKER_01]: I mended my ways and so on.

75
00:06:54,394 --> 00:07:05,982
[SPEAKER_01]: It's more like on the level of lying to yourself and living in that mode of not actually being celibate, but convincing yourself that you are.

76
00:07:07,208 --> 00:07:13,850
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you see, it's not like... in and of itself, obviously, that doesn't harm anybody, so to speak.

77
00:07:13,930 --> 00:07:27,135
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not like killing somebody or stealing or lying, but you could see the weight of that existential deception, self-imposed, self-created, self-maintained, that is gonna completely, like, mess up

78
00:07:27,950 --> 00:07:30,471
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, it's on the basis of your choice.

79
00:07:48,234 --> 00:07:56,738
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means a person can technically start choosing to become transparent and question and re-evaluate what they believe themselves are or not.

80
00:07:57,458 --> 00:07:59,019
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it can be undone, in other words.

81
00:08:00,219 --> 00:08:05,762
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you have the second type of person who, without grounds, as it says, accuses a person who is celibate of not being celibate.

82
00:08:06,842 --> 00:08:08,723
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that would be like a... You could see this.

83
00:08:08,743 --> 00:08:10,764
[SPEAKER_01]: You could ask yourself, like, why would somebody do that?

84
00:08:11,864 --> 00:08:16,286
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what scenario would kind of prompt somebody to do that?

85
00:08:16,386 --> 00:08:18,047
[SPEAKER_01]: To say, oh, you know,

86
00:08:19,594 --> 00:08:23,895
[SPEAKER_01]: He who is celibate, I want to accuse him of not being celibate.

87
00:08:23,935 --> 00:08:27,796
[SPEAKER_01]: I want others to think that he's not celibate.

88
00:08:28,957 --> 00:08:40,540
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, of course, you could have some individual instances, because they fell out or something, but this speaks on the level of the principle of somebody engaging with, as we can see with the third one, engaging on the level of the view.

89
00:08:41,545 --> 00:08:49,632
[SPEAKER_01]: That, oh, I'm not celibate, but I undertaken the claim of being celibate, and I believe I'm celibate, but actually my actions keep contradicting it, and I'm turning a blind eye to it.

90
00:08:50,233 --> 00:08:56,218
[SPEAKER_01]: The second one would be, oh, celibacy basically frightens me.

91
00:08:56,258 --> 00:09:01,263
[SPEAKER_01]: The idea of giving up sensuality is like the abyss, like how the other... when that layman was talking to the Buddha.

92
00:09:02,237 --> 00:09:06,179
[SPEAKER_01]: It says when the householders hear about restraint, it's like a sudden abyss.

93
00:09:06,920 --> 00:09:09,861
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like, that's fine in and of itself.

94
00:09:10,282 --> 00:09:13,383
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, experiencing that, it's normal.

95
00:09:13,924 --> 00:09:19,147
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, somebody who depends on sensuality hearing that they have to abandon it, that will be the most natural kind of response.

96
00:09:19,207 --> 00:09:20,347
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh my God, this is frightening.

97
00:09:21,388 --> 00:09:22,309
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's not a problem.

98
00:09:22,349 --> 00:09:28,212
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is what view you then choose to, pretty much, develop and fall into,

99
00:09:29,588 --> 00:09:35,874
[SPEAKER_01]: So that you obscure and cover up and protect yourself from that fear and justify not actually doing anything about it.

100
00:09:36,615 --> 00:09:51,870
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you could see how then somebody would hear about celibacy, hear about the peril of sensuality, hear about that if you want to make merit and be reborn in heavens and so on and believes in that, like, they need to.

101
00:09:52,670 --> 00:10:00,354
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, put a lid on the degree of animal behavior they engage with and also the reasons behind engaging with.

102
00:10:00,394 --> 00:10:01,494
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't be just careless.

103
00:10:01,895 --> 00:10:03,275
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's allowed and stuff like that.

104
00:10:04,416 --> 00:10:07,337
[SPEAKER_01]: But then you could see like, oh, that feels like too much of a task.

105
00:10:08,358 --> 00:10:12,360
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the easiest, the first sort of port of call would be, oh,

106
00:10:13,308 --> 00:10:14,849
[SPEAKER_01]: Blame those that are able to do it.

107
00:10:15,369 --> 00:10:18,510
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you kind of, deep down, feel like you're not able to do it.

108
00:10:18,570 --> 00:10:19,410
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't want to do it.

109
00:10:19,430 --> 00:10:20,470
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't want to have to go through it.

110
00:10:20,911 --> 00:10:25,172
[SPEAKER_01]: So, let me find fault in those that are doing it and say, no, even they can't do it.

111
00:10:25,932 --> 00:10:26,913
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody can really do it.

112
00:10:26,953 --> 00:10:28,053
[SPEAKER_01]: They just claim they do it.

113
00:10:28,713 --> 00:10:31,674
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're not really even care about them that much.

114
00:10:31,714 --> 00:10:39,377
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just doing that... You're just doing that to protect yourself.

115
00:10:40,542 --> 00:10:51,027
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, to put the view outside, that they can be shared by others, and partaken others, and affirmed by others, acknowledged by others, validated by others, that, oh, yeah, nobody can really do it.

116
00:10:51,107 --> 00:11:00,371
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, I am exempted from feeling that I must also do it, which then increases that initial fear that I experienced when I heard about the sensory strain in the abyss.

117
00:11:02,032 --> 00:11:04,733
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, the third person would be like taking that view

118
00:11:06,471 --> 00:11:21,099
[SPEAKER_01]: Even, like, say, not directly directing it on anybody personally, to justify their non-doing, but then just pertaining, making a statement and developing a belief on the level of a view, which is that, no, sensuality is not an obstacle, basically.

119
00:11:21,900 --> 00:11:28,723
[SPEAKER_01]: So, eh... they don't even try to take it on, and then fail, and maybe delude themselves, and maybe rectify that.

120
00:11:29,304 --> 00:11:34,767
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't even try to, like, oh, let me undermine those that claim it's possible,

121
00:11:36,261 --> 00:11:38,001
[SPEAKER_01]: And say that it's not possible.

122
00:11:38,021 --> 00:11:44,403
[SPEAKER_01]: No, they just say... Now, the whole view that sensuality is a problem, it's not really a problem.

123
00:11:44,823 --> 00:11:46,343
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is no fault in sensual pleasure.

124
00:11:46,383 --> 00:11:49,164
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why this one is the worst, actually.

125
00:11:49,184 --> 00:11:52,364
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you can see, it's the most personal, in a sense.

126
00:11:52,424 --> 00:11:58,586
[SPEAKER_01]: It only really involves you, doesn't involve your public statement, how others perceive you, doesn't inform what others do, but it's actually the worst.

127
00:11:59,186 --> 00:12:00,166
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it kind of

128
00:12:01,201 --> 00:12:12,541
[SPEAKER_01]: It pertains to self-delusion and obscuring and covering up the most obvious, which is that, you know, if you're honest, even of the most hedonistic person, you know, in the moments of their clarity,

129
00:12:13,708 --> 00:12:24,751
[SPEAKER_01]: You could see the kind of the deprivation, the unpleasantness, the unpleasant drive and force of their own desires, that they're just subjected to.

130
00:12:25,491 --> 00:12:30,213
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, you would get fatigued, you would get put off, if you just honestly look at it.

131
00:12:30,713 --> 00:12:34,834
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, of course, you know, you might decide to ignore it, and most people would, and so on.

132
00:12:35,214 --> 00:12:38,455
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's one thing is to, okay, I can't deal with this, I don't want to do anything about it.

133
00:12:38,895 --> 00:12:42,216
[SPEAKER_01]: But another thing is like, oh no, let me develop a view.

134
00:12:44,171 --> 00:12:47,594
[SPEAKER_01]: They will soothe and comfort my own self-imposed delusion.

135
00:12:47,634 --> 00:12:56,062
[SPEAKER_01]: They will then maintain that whole kind of sense of ease that I have, in terms of, oh, I don't have to deal with this.

136
00:12:56,142 --> 00:12:59,585
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't have to go through restraint or withdrawal, because there is no fault in this anyway.

137
00:13:00,587 --> 00:13:11,538
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it says, because of their view, they will become even more careless in regard to sensuality than they would have been otherwise, even without having, even without, say, being celibate and withdrawn.

138
00:13:12,059 --> 00:13:18,245
[SPEAKER_01]: So, somebody who kind of, it's on a somewhat, has some sort of recognition of

139
00:13:19,530 --> 00:13:30,756
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you know, it's good to sort of at least moderate your sensuality and engagement in how much time you sort of dwell on it, how far you let your mind take you.

140
00:13:30,796 --> 00:13:35,619
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, yeah, it's probably good to sort of restrain it a bit, even in the worldly terms.

141
00:13:35,639 --> 00:13:45,364
[SPEAKER_01]: We're not even talking about the practice of the dhamma now, because it's just about, you know, whether you're going in the state of deprivation or you're going upwards in heavens or better rivers.

142
00:13:45,925 --> 00:13:46,385
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like,

143
00:13:48,133 --> 00:14:00,376
[SPEAKER_01]: Without, like as I said, even without abandoning sensuality, but at least not completely covering up kind of the obvious course, immediately verifiable nature of when desire arises, that it's actually unpleasant and that whole thing.

144
00:14:01,913 --> 00:14:07,018
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't be as bad, because it will at least keep you somewhat in check when it comes to sensuality.

145
00:14:07,438 --> 00:14:22,553
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you actually, after having heard that sensuality is bad and stuff, you actually choose to go the other way and double down, and convince yourself that no, there is no fault in sensuality, you start to override even that basic recognition, which now removed any breaks you had.

146
00:14:23,562 --> 00:14:31,227
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means now you would really not think twice about engaging in sensual pleasures because you have successfully convinced yourself that there's absolutely no fault in it.

147
00:14:32,167 --> 00:14:37,371
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why this is on the level of, yeah, you're destined for the states of deprivation.

148
00:14:38,291 --> 00:14:41,013
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why it's like...

149
00:14:41,253 --> 00:14:52,420
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not that, you know, say a lay practitioner, somebody who wants to practice, is destined for the state of deprivation simply by not being celibate or something.

150
00:14:54,044 --> 00:15:02,210
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, no, you need to... One thing is to kind of train and develop and fail and try again through that transparency and recognition with the practices.

151
00:15:02,590 --> 00:15:11,717
[SPEAKER_01]: Another thing is to just, like, fault-find, so to undermine stuff that you heard from others that you should do in terms of practice.

152
00:15:11,857 --> 00:15:16,680
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, no, well, look, they don't do it themselves, as if that kind of absolves you from doing it.

153
00:15:16,900 --> 00:15:20,623
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, even worse, to say, no, there's actually... this doesn't need to be abandoned.

154
00:15:20,723 --> 00:15:22,024
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no problem with this.

155
00:15:24,787 --> 00:15:26,550
[SPEAKER_01]: Which, as I said, overrides the basic recognition.

156
00:15:26,670 --> 00:15:33,801
[SPEAKER_01]: And, obviously, in the case of somebody who is familiar with the suttas, it's kind of even worse, because they override what the Buddha himself said in so many suttas.

157
00:15:35,384 --> 00:15:36,926
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, it's like, one thing is to say...

158
00:15:37,936 --> 00:15:40,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, maybe, you know, it feels too much now.

159
00:15:40,678 --> 00:15:48,602
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe I need to work for it gradually and then start working towards right restraint, right intention and purifying your behavior.

160
00:15:49,062 --> 00:15:52,644
[SPEAKER_01]: Another thing is to say, oh, no, no, that was just for the monks.

161
00:15:52,684 --> 00:15:53,765
[SPEAKER_01]: That doesn't apply to me.

162
00:15:53,865 --> 00:15:56,426
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, at the same time, I claim I follow the Buddhist teaching.

163
00:15:57,640 --> 00:16:04,824
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's just, you could see, like, the layers and layers of extra, kind of, perversions and contradictions added to the obvious truth.

164
00:16:05,004 --> 00:16:05,544
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, to obvious.

165
00:16:05,584 --> 00:16:08,946
[SPEAKER_01]: Anybody honest enough to admit truth in this, would arrive at that conclusion.

166
00:16:11,027 --> 00:16:12,048
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why it's like, eh...

167
00:16:14,288 --> 00:16:15,188
[SPEAKER_01]: This is kind of useful.

168
00:16:15,208 --> 00:16:42,504
[SPEAKER_01]: I included this sutta because, like, leading to the next one that I wanted to talk about, it was kind of just to recognize that the training of, like, freeing yourself from your desires, being able to contain them and free yourself, it's not like something you just can adhere to externally, even if, as I said before, you keep all the precepts perfectly, but don't make the effort to actually train in those principles, you still remain bound with the same desires.

169
00:16:43,270 --> 00:16:44,251
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just not acting out of them.

170
00:16:44,331 --> 00:16:45,432
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's good, as the Buddha said.

171
00:16:45,492 --> 00:16:55,779
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if somebody spends the rest of... monk spends the rest of his life just crying with pains of restraint, but doesn't go back to those desires, he'll still heading upwards.

172
00:16:55,819 --> 00:16:57,320
[SPEAKER_01]: He's still heading to heavens, at least.

173
00:16:58,040 --> 00:16:59,121
[SPEAKER_01]: If he doesn't get the right view.

174
00:17:00,722 --> 00:17:03,704
[SPEAKER_01]: But obviously, you know, more needs to be done.

175
00:17:04,565 --> 00:17:06,646
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's that whole training that we often talk about.

176
00:17:06,806 --> 00:17:08,107
[SPEAKER_01]: Recognizing your intentions and stuff.

177
00:17:08,127 --> 00:17:11,930
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not just a question of saying no, blanket no, to everything that your mind ever wants.

178
00:17:13,198 --> 00:17:26,143
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's like the second sutta, like the three origins for the arising of action, and it's like when the desire arises for the past phenomena, future phenomena, or the present phenomena.

179
00:17:26,784 --> 00:17:28,924
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that is what's considered an action.

180
00:17:29,044 --> 00:17:33,326
[SPEAKER_01]: So, acting out of a desire, it's what kamma is, as we said before.

181
00:17:34,667 --> 00:17:37,648
[SPEAKER_01]: So, and how does the desire arise?

182
00:17:38,592 --> 00:17:49,230
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, when, say, a phenomena concerning past, future or present, doesn't matter what thoughts or what perceptions would have come up, that can serve as grounds for desire and lust,

183
00:17:50,032 --> 00:18:10,522
[SPEAKER_01]: And, you know, certain things universally serve for... as grounds for desire and lust for everybody, but for different people there's going to be some kind of idiosyncratic variations, like something will not be lustful for somebody due to their own history, culture upbringing, but for somebody else will be, due to your past actions as well, like what you've been choosing so far and so on.

184
00:18:10,542 --> 00:18:16,005
[SPEAKER_01]: The lust grows and proliferates and gets associated with new things or disassociated from some other things.

185
00:18:16,882 --> 00:18:20,244
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the point is not to examine the nature of those phenomena.

186
00:18:20,284 --> 00:18:23,566
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is to recognize whatever phenomena has arisen.

187
00:18:24,107 --> 00:18:26,808
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there a corresponding possibility of lust?

188
00:18:26,888 --> 00:18:27,869
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there an incentive?

189
00:18:27,989 --> 00:18:29,430
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there a pressure towards lust?

190
00:18:29,570 --> 00:18:30,871
[SPEAKER_01]: That already qualifies enough.

191
00:18:30,951 --> 00:18:36,234
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to know more about the type and the content and how this phenomena came to be and so on.

192
00:18:36,574 --> 00:18:38,155
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all pretty much irrelevant.

193
00:18:39,456 --> 00:18:40,957
[SPEAKER_01]: So, eh...

194
00:18:43,675 --> 00:18:50,039
[SPEAKER_01]: With reference to past, future, present phenomena, the grounds for desire and lust, one repeatedly thinks and ponders them along the same lines with the mind.

195
00:18:51,100 --> 00:18:52,081
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the key here.

196
00:18:52,701 --> 00:18:59,045
[SPEAKER_01]: You have perceptions arising, whether Saitama's mental states touches thoughts in regard to past, future or present, doesn't matter.

197
00:19:00,186 --> 00:19:06,731
[SPEAKER_01]: As I said, they come up with a... with a... with an offer, with a promise, with a... with a push in a certain directions.

198
00:19:06,931 --> 00:19:08,432
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you don't necessarily know

199
00:19:09,636 --> 00:19:19,920
[SPEAKER_01]: Where or what specifically your desire wants you to do or have or perceive, you can already tell it's... it wants... it's going to be something.

200
00:19:20,420 --> 00:19:21,701
[SPEAKER_01]: It's sending you in a direction.

201
00:19:21,881 --> 00:19:23,281
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the pressure it's already felt.

202
00:19:24,182 --> 00:19:25,662
[SPEAKER_01]: That's, like, unmistakable.

203
00:19:26,102 --> 00:19:27,083
[SPEAKER_01]: You already know that.

204
00:19:27,883 --> 00:19:32,565
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whenever something pressuring arises, that it's sort of, kind of,

205
00:19:33,907 --> 00:19:36,928
[SPEAKER_01]: You're pushing for the thoughts, for the momentum behind the thoughts.

206
00:19:37,868 --> 00:19:40,008
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, let me let me go with this and think about it.

207
00:19:40,188 --> 00:19:44,669
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what this means, that one repeatedly thinks and ponders them along the same lines.

208
00:19:45,469 --> 00:19:52,071
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not like, oh, something arises and I reflect on it, or I just broadly, abstractly think about it.

209
00:19:52,091 --> 00:20:01,072
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, no, when it's coming out of that thread of compulsion, even before it's defined as compulsion, necessarily, because the pressure is already a form of compulsion.

210
00:20:01,833 --> 00:20:03,273
[SPEAKER_01]: So, are your thoughts

211
00:20:05,814 --> 00:20:10,356
[SPEAKER_01]: Past, future, present, phenomena, doesn't matter, connected.

212
00:20:10,496 --> 00:20:19,502
[SPEAKER_01]: Are they infused and are they sort of exhibiting that pressure on you to continue thinking along the lines that they presented themselves with?

213
00:20:20,700 --> 00:20:34,209
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's, you know, positive... positive in a sense of, like, desirable, agreeable, to think more, ponder, engage with it, speak about it, expose your senses to it, and so on.

214
00:20:34,649 --> 00:20:38,892
[SPEAKER_01]: Or even worse, it works the same way in the opposite, like, if it's... if it's aversive.

215
00:20:39,272 --> 00:20:39,493
[SPEAKER_01]: Same.

216
00:20:39,593 --> 00:20:47,498
[SPEAKER_01]: The pressure rises for aversion to go along the lines, along the lines of aversion that's already implied, that that phenomenon is pushing you towards.

217
00:20:48,700 --> 00:21:00,065
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's that middle ground, that right level I often talk about, that people need to discern, and the only way to discern that is to first stop automatically acting out of these things.

218
00:21:00,125 --> 00:21:04,446
[SPEAKER_01]: Acting out of desire towards more of it, and then acting out of aversion towards trying to get rid of the entire experience.

219
00:21:04,626 --> 00:21:15,731
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, trying to deny, remove, distract yourself away, concentrate yourself away from the present phenomena, because you don't see the difference between present phenomena that are grounds for desire,

220
00:21:16,211 --> 00:21:18,914
[SPEAKER_01]: And the actual desire and lust, that is pretty much your doing.

221
00:21:20,015 --> 00:21:20,716
[SPEAKER_01]: How is your doing?

222
00:21:20,776 --> 00:21:27,503
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you repeatedly allow, think and ponder, choose to go along the lines that they offer.

223
00:21:29,124 --> 00:21:32,287
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what happens when you don't go along the lines that they offer?

224
00:21:32,347 --> 00:21:34,450
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the lines remain there, not followed.

225
00:21:35,190 --> 00:21:37,012
[SPEAKER_01]: The pressure remains there, dried up.

226
00:21:38,474 --> 00:21:39,695
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you see through it.

227
00:21:41,862 --> 00:21:54,606
[SPEAKER_01]: So, while one is repeatedly thinking and pondering them along the same lines, desire will grow, it will arise, it will become even more associated with those phenomena, and mind, the animal, will be completely fettered by that bond.

228
00:21:55,706 --> 00:22:01,628
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, whatever you do in response to that, go on a retreat, sense deprivation,

229
00:22:02,837 --> 00:22:07,561
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that is to manage the past, future, present phenomena and possibility of their arising.

230
00:22:07,761 --> 00:22:12,385
[SPEAKER_01]: None of that addresses the mind's lust, the desire towards those things.

231
00:22:12,405 --> 00:22:13,505
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like starving the animal.

232
00:22:13,986 --> 00:22:18,329
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot keep it still, you cannot train it, you cannot teach it to only eat the right food.

233
00:22:19,150 --> 00:22:22,893
[SPEAKER_01]: All you can do it let it eat or completely starve it.

234
00:22:23,709 --> 00:22:34,239
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you starve it on a retreat, on a super intense concentration, nostril watching practice, or whatever other kind of methods of denial you might engage with.

235
00:22:34,719 --> 00:22:44,609
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, when that wears out, and it will, the animal still has no knowledge, no difference between eating things that are not the ground for desire, and eating things and engaging with things that are the ground for desire.

236
00:22:44,629 --> 00:22:46,010
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the mind needs to learn that.

237
00:22:47,572 --> 00:22:47,772
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

238
00:22:48,890 --> 00:22:52,953
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how desire arises, and then the Buddha goes to describe how desire does not arise.

239
00:22:53,013 --> 00:22:58,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, one understands the subsequent results of past and future present phenomena that are ground for desire and lust.

240
00:22:58,557 --> 00:23:04,722
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how, because does desire not arise with regard to past, future, present phenomena that are ground for it?

241
00:23:05,542 --> 00:23:08,725
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, one understands the subsequent results that are ground for it.

242
00:23:08,985 --> 00:23:15,910
[SPEAKER_01]: So, one now understands the implicit results that follow from this, that are implicit in it.

243
00:23:16,683 --> 00:23:19,707
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like, I have to go through subsequent results to understand this.

244
00:23:19,767 --> 00:23:20,107
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, no.

245
00:23:20,248 --> 00:23:21,609
[SPEAKER_01]: One presently understands that.

246
00:23:23,291 --> 00:23:25,975
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how one turns away from this desire now.

247
00:23:26,802 --> 00:23:27,902
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what the Sutta describes.

248
00:23:28,083 --> 00:23:33,665
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like, yeah, go through it until you're jaded and then you can tell yourself, see, I'm desireless now.

249
00:23:33,945 --> 00:23:34,785
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you're just tired.

250
00:23:35,426 --> 00:23:38,127
[SPEAKER_01]: Your animal is asleep because he got what he wanted.

251
00:23:38,147 --> 00:23:39,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe even sick because he's over eight.

252
00:23:40,128 --> 00:23:44,650
[SPEAKER_01]: But, again, that will go away and it's going to go back to the same things because he hasn't learned.

253
00:23:45,510 --> 00:23:52,653
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's understanding the implicit results of the phenomena that are ground for desire and lust right now.

254
00:23:53,433 --> 00:23:56,877
[SPEAKER_01]: Understanding what they imply, way before you end up there.

255
00:23:58,038 --> 00:23:59,760
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how the mind turns away from them.

256
00:24:00,360 --> 00:24:02,903
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how desire will not grow.

257
00:24:03,083 --> 00:24:06,447
[SPEAKER_01]: It will diminish and it will not become associated now with those phenomena.

258
00:24:07,561 --> 00:24:13,403
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the practical examples of this would be, well, pretty much any reflection done rightly.

259
00:24:13,903 --> 00:24:22,066
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you know, the reflection is like a sub-reflection or even, as I said, even, like, in terms of the ill-will and so on, you could undermine the same thing.

260
00:24:22,826 --> 00:24:33,710
[SPEAKER_01]: If you also, well, if you first don't follow immediately along the lines of the pressure, along the, well, the lines that pressure kind of offers you,

261
00:24:35,321 --> 00:24:37,302
[SPEAKER_01]: To think about, to follow along.

262
00:24:37,882 --> 00:24:44,346
[SPEAKER_01]: You say no to that, but then you don't deny the pressure, because again, you need to, as the Sutta says later, you need to penetrate through it.

263
00:24:44,366 --> 00:24:45,286
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to understand it.

264
00:24:45,846 --> 00:24:48,447
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just replace it with something else that suits you.

265
00:24:49,888 --> 00:24:52,810
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the problem is not past, future, present phenomena that are ground for lust.

266
00:24:52,850 --> 00:24:57,332
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is that whenever they're present, the mind cannot but help it fall into lust.

267
00:24:58,206 --> 00:25:04,590
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to teach the mind in regard to those prompts, in regard to those things, not just get rid of them, have something else there.

268
00:25:05,370 --> 00:25:20,359
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the subsequent results, the implicit results, could be anything, from, like, well, as the sutra says, like, in some sutra, like, the danger of sensuality is having to work and plow fields, having to earn, having to protect what you earn, being exposed to the elements, having to deal with other people.

269
00:25:20,399 --> 00:25:21,219
[SPEAKER_01]: Those are the dangers.

270
00:25:21,279 --> 00:25:22,080
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's like, yeah, they are.

271
00:25:22,520 --> 00:25:25,382
[SPEAKER_01]: They are the implicit results, even if it's like we're looking 20 years later.

272
00:25:26,327 --> 00:25:28,288
[SPEAKER_01]: But, whatever works on different times.

273
00:25:29,249 --> 00:25:36,894
[SPEAKER_01]: If that's what your desire aims, well, those will be the subsequent implicit results that become revealed when you don't follow along the lines automatically.

274
00:25:39,216 --> 00:25:47,562
[SPEAKER_01]: If, on the other hand, you don't think that far, you could bring it to the immediate, such as the natural disgust of the body.

275
00:25:48,082 --> 00:25:55,588
[SPEAKER_01]: Say, in terms of the sexual desire, which is already like a form of consumption, like, not acting out of it,

276
00:25:56,351 --> 00:26:00,735
[SPEAKER_01]: Not trying to deny that there is a, again, mind leading towards lust.

277
00:26:01,175 --> 00:26:07,060
[SPEAKER_01]: Not following the thoughts, fantasies that are outlined there, the directions of those.

278
00:26:07,080 --> 00:26:09,682
[SPEAKER_01]: Not allowing in the center stage, as I said in previous talks.

279
00:26:10,743 --> 00:26:24,754
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, instead, choosing to think, again, within the same setup of past, future, present, phenomena, ground for desire, you choose to think the non- the factually non-desiring aspects at the same time.

280
00:26:24,794 --> 00:26:25,595
[SPEAKER_01]: So, concurrently.

281
00:26:26,510 --> 00:26:36,935
[SPEAKER_01]: So, one of the implicit results of a more immediate kind would be consuming or engaging with the form that's subjectively disgusting.

282
00:26:37,876 --> 00:26:40,157
[SPEAKER_01]: And you would do that if you ignore the disgusting aspects.

283
00:26:40,837 --> 00:26:51,943
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you have the disgusting aspects there, again, not as a replacement to the past, future, present phenomena, but as a kind of additional context.

284
00:26:53,068 --> 00:26:54,188
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'm not trying to replace.

285
00:26:54,268 --> 00:26:57,469
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm just trying to include it at the same time and let the mind decide.

286
00:26:59,110 --> 00:27:00,010
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the key here.

287
00:27:00,030 --> 00:27:01,070
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the right balance.

288
00:27:01,130 --> 00:27:21,637
[SPEAKER_01]: The right balance is setting up a subha, setting up non-ill will, not as a replacement, as a force to replace and change the mind, but as something so that, even enough with the pressures towards lust or towards ill will, even enough so that the mind can see both equally and then it will naturally actually prefer then,

289
00:27:22,717 --> 00:27:23,878
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, this is not desirable.

290
00:27:23,938 --> 00:27:31,081
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the only reason things are desirable is because the non-desirable aspects are ignored.

291
00:27:31,101 --> 00:27:37,324
[SPEAKER_01]: Or are thought about after, as an afterthought, not presented and maintained on the same right level.

292
00:27:38,667 --> 00:27:51,235
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it doesn't kind of seep through the association that, oh, any desire, any involvement with the desirable sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, is inseparable.

293
00:27:51,395 --> 00:28:04,604
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the same thing as these disgusting organs and brain and bile and excrement and all these other reflections that, you know, that monks are encouraged to do, or anybody who wants to free themselves from sensuality should be doing.

294
00:28:05,164 --> 00:28:15,793
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, not as an empty practice, mantra, mechanical that you go through, but as something that... Sure, you might think of it like when you have no desire.

295
00:28:15,833 --> 00:28:16,373
[SPEAKER_01]: Think about it.

296
00:28:16,934 --> 00:28:20,597
[SPEAKER_01]: Think about it in that really palpable sense of these disgusting aspects.

297
00:28:21,057 --> 00:28:30,885
[SPEAKER_01]: But the point is, so that you can be able to, that when the mind is aroused or pressured with a desire, you're able to also bring up this context.

298
00:28:31,722 --> 00:28:34,924
[SPEAKER_01]: And keep it there for the mind, until the mind calms down.

299
00:28:35,344 --> 00:28:35,785
[SPEAKER_01]: And it will.

300
00:28:36,405 --> 00:28:36,985
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you know.

301
00:28:37,066 --> 00:28:38,206
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't need to get rid of the mind.

302
00:28:38,627 --> 00:28:39,867
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't need to destroy the world.

303
00:28:39,927 --> 00:28:43,049
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't need to pull my eyes out to never see anything agreeable.

304
00:28:43,930 --> 00:28:57,419
[SPEAKER_01]: I just need to never willingly choose to go along the default lines of the pressure, and then be lazy in terms of bringing up the other counterpart, the other side of the same coin, at the same time.

305
00:28:57,519 --> 00:28:58,640
[SPEAKER_01]: Just to that extent.

306
00:28:59,100 --> 00:29:00,421
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't need to force it further.

307
00:29:01,362 --> 00:29:09,448
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you might feel like you start bringing up the asubha to the right extent, and you see, like, the mind just completely losing the desire, and then you feel like, okay, let me push it harder.

308
00:29:09,828 --> 00:29:11,789
[SPEAKER_01]: Let me give it more asubha now.

309
00:29:11,849 --> 00:29:13,751
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, no, now you're falling from the tune.

310
00:29:14,431 --> 00:29:15,892
[SPEAKER_01]: You're falling from the right music.

311
00:29:16,272 --> 00:29:17,193
[SPEAKER_01]: Now it's getting too tight.

312
00:29:18,544 --> 00:29:19,865
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where you have to stay patient.

313
00:29:19,885 --> 00:29:20,506
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, no, no, no.

314
00:29:21,086 --> 00:29:22,928
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll keep it only... but then the mind will come up.

315
00:29:23,008 --> 00:29:32,255
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the mind will come up again and I'll again set up the right balance and maintain it until the mind learns that this thing that it wants is actually this thing.

316
00:29:32,275 --> 00:29:40,903
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just the one aspect of this fundamentally repulsive, fundamentally non-desirable in that way thing.

317
00:29:41,183 --> 00:29:41,563
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

318
00:29:41,924 --> 00:29:43,545
[SPEAKER_01]: That's to the extent that you need to do it.

319
00:29:44,825 --> 00:29:48,227
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's how... and that's also, see, an implicit result.

320
00:29:48,648 --> 00:29:56,193
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if I now allow these thoughts or desires, the implicit result of that is me consuming the excrement.

321
00:29:56,553 --> 00:29:58,855
[SPEAKER_01]: Me being in contact with bile.

322
00:29:59,075 --> 00:30:03,278
[SPEAKER_01]: Me with being factually, like, in touch with things that I wouldn't touch otherwise.

323
00:30:03,758 --> 00:30:06,700
[SPEAKER_01]: Even as that other sutta says, when that man was offering his daughter to the Buddha.

324
00:30:07,141 --> 00:30:08,882
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I wouldn't touch that form with a foot.

325
00:30:09,602 --> 00:30:10,163
[SPEAKER_01]: With my foot.

326
00:30:10,283 --> 00:30:11,864
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, like, what do you want me to do with it?

327
00:30:12,868 --> 00:30:22,215
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like, you factually wouldn't do it, if it's presented like a... like a... like a non... like, you know, organs, bile, the... the stuff that body is made of.

328
00:30:22,775 --> 00:30:35,384
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's another subsequent result of my engagement is me actually being in contact, being in touch, being bound with... with stuff that I factually, in isolation, would deem repulsive.

329
00:30:36,405 --> 00:30:38,667
[SPEAKER_01]: Would deem like, oh, what was I thinking, type of thing.

330
00:30:39,480 --> 00:30:53,725
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to have that context present, when there is that offer of, oh, think along these lines, just relax and go along this, give the mind the fantasy it wants, or, you know, if you're not engaging in the bodily or verbal behavior, but still, now the fantasy becomes the expression of it.

331
00:30:53,745 --> 00:30:55,506
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like, no, I gotta counter that as well.

332
00:30:56,886 --> 00:31:00,208
[SPEAKER_01]: Instead of going along those lines, I'll maintain these lines.

333
00:31:00,248 --> 00:31:01,449
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll maintain these perceptions.

334
00:31:01,589 --> 00:31:08,033
[SPEAKER_01]: Just, again, I'm not expecting any instantaneous kind of insight and resolution.

335
00:31:08,193 --> 00:31:14,497
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm just keeping it there to make sure the mind knows that, well, if you want this, this has to be there with it.

336
00:31:14,677 --> 00:31:15,898
[SPEAKER_01]: It cannot not be there.

337
00:31:17,441 --> 00:31:26,165
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the diligence, that's the practice that one does, that then results in there will be no desire and lust spreading, growing, and the arisen one will diminish.

338
00:31:27,386 --> 00:31:36,890
[SPEAKER_01]: Without you needing to deal, distract yourself away, concentrate yourself away from any past, future, present traumas, memories, preferences, doesn't matter.

339
00:31:37,110 --> 00:31:38,771
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that is addressed presently,

340
00:31:39,670 --> 00:31:42,411
[SPEAKER_01]: Through diminishing and uprooting the desire towards it.

341
00:31:42,712 --> 00:31:47,294
[SPEAKER_01]: Towards having it, towards dealing with it, towards getting rid of it, towards whatever, basically.

342
00:31:47,334 --> 00:31:48,955
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever direction your mind wants to take.

343
00:31:54,738 --> 00:31:56,278
[SPEAKER_01]: So, let me just see...

344
00:31:57,959 --> 00:31:59,160
[SPEAKER_00]: Were there any more questions?

345
00:32:02,677 --> 00:32:18,828
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, I think I kind of mentioned, like, a touch upon it, maybe even I spoke about it more, but basically, yeah, like, so why, for example, when it says past phenomena, future phenomena, present phenomena, why is the only way of penetrating them, understanding them, here and now, in the present?

346
00:32:20,390 --> 00:32:22,211
[SPEAKER_01]: Because desire and lust are always in the present.

347
00:32:24,312 --> 00:32:26,614
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if you think about your past desire and lust,

348
00:32:28,515 --> 00:32:36,983
[SPEAKER_01]: If now you have desire on account of you thinking about your past engagement with phenomena and objects of desire and lust, that is your present desire.

349
00:32:37,023 --> 00:32:37,223
[SPEAKER_01]: Same.

350
00:32:37,744 --> 00:32:43,089
[SPEAKER_01]: Thinking about the future, planning you want it now, that's your present desire.

351
00:32:43,229 --> 00:32:43,930
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your action.

352
00:32:44,230 --> 00:32:47,133
[SPEAKER_01]: So, thinking about the future is not a problem.

353
00:32:47,173 --> 00:32:51,117
[SPEAKER_01]: Past that you've been through, that you cannot change, is not a problem.

354
00:32:52,404 --> 00:32:58,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Having still desire or aversion even, now, that's where the connection is still maintained.

355
00:32:58,331 --> 00:32:59,652
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where the problem still exists.

356
00:33:00,293 --> 00:33:01,875
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you remove the desire now.

357
00:33:03,356 --> 00:33:04,757
[SPEAKER_01]: You would have overcome your entire past.

358
00:33:04,857 --> 00:33:08,278
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter how wild would have been, could have been, and so on.

359
00:33:08,998 --> 00:33:09,718
[SPEAKER_01]: Same with the future.

360
00:33:09,738 --> 00:33:11,939
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter how insecure or uncertain.

361
00:33:12,539 --> 00:33:15,861
[SPEAKER_01]: It won't affect you now, because there is no desire to interfere with it.

362
00:33:16,301 --> 00:33:17,561
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no desire to change it.

363
00:33:17,661 --> 00:33:24,584
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no desire to address it, because, well, you understood that the problem was never in those phenomena that are ground for lust.

364
00:33:25,204 --> 00:33:28,265
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem was in your own currently maintained desire towards it.

365
00:33:29,186 --> 00:33:32,127
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I did ask, like, what does it mean to penetrate it with the mind?

366
00:33:33,174 --> 00:33:34,374
[SPEAKER_01]: And, actually, I answered it.

367
00:33:35,055 --> 00:33:37,536
[SPEAKER_01]: It's obviously, like, people answer that rightly.

368
00:33:37,556 --> 00:33:40,557
[SPEAKER_01]: It's, like, what means that the animal needs to understand it.

369
00:33:41,277 --> 00:33:44,218
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not just about you figuring it out.

370
00:33:45,058 --> 00:34:02,025
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's, like, another kind of, say, another reason why I often say contemporary practices, meditation techniques, actually take you in a completely different direction, in opposite direction of where you should be going.

371
00:34:03,532 --> 00:34:07,835
[SPEAKER_01]: Anytime you think, well, concentration helps and helps me focus, helps me calm down.

372
00:34:09,315 --> 00:34:12,637
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that pertains to you and your choice of attention.

373
00:34:13,358 --> 00:34:16,920
[SPEAKER_01]: What you choose to attend, what you choose to ignore.

374
00:34:17,580 --> 00:34:22,303
[SPEAKER_01]: None of that results in animal actually getting the right message.

375
00:34:23,703 --> 00:34:26,225
[SPEAKER_01]: In animal actually understanding for itself.

376
00:34:27,441 --> 00:34:38,762
[SPEAKER_01]: Because your focusing away, your concentration, aims, as I said already, aims at dealing with phenomena that are ground for lust.

377
00:34:40,049 --> 00:34:46,831
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you know, practice mindfulness, body sensations, focus here and now, don't think about it.

378
00:34:46,851 --> 00:34:49,972
[SPEAKER_00]: Or often, as you hear in the contemporary tradition, it's like, well, you know,

379
00:34:58,796 --> 00:34:59,736
[SPEAKER_01]: Future is not yet.

380
00:34:59,816 --> 00:35:01,097
[SPEAKER_01]: So, stop thinking about it.

381
00:35:01,278 --> 00:35:02,258
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't dwell on the past.

382
00:35:02,298 --> 00:35:03,199
[SPEAKER_01]: Just let go of it.

383
00:35:03,339 --> 00:35:04,840
[SPEAKER_01]: As if past and future are the problem.

384
00:35:05,101 --> 00:35:06,201
[SPEAKER_01]: Those thoughts are the problem.

385
00:35:06,281 --> 00:35:07,102
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do I deal with it?

386
00:35:07,202 --> 00:35:09,564
[SPEAKER_01]: I deal with it by concentrating on the present moment, present nostril.

387
00:35:10,245 --> 00:35:13,187
[SPEAKER_01]: When that's not enough, you need to increase the present moment.

388
00:35:13,988 --> 00:35:15,609
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to make it even more specific.

389
00:35:16,480 --> 00:35:36,136
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not incidental that all the Abhidhammas and and similar views, similar meditation techniques and practices evolved in that direction to the point of now, you are seeing imaginary kalapas that don't really exist, they exist in your fantasy, as the real measures of objective experience that is sub-atomic level of, like, your perception.

390
00:35:36,156 --> 00:35:37,297
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just complete nonsense.

391
00:35:37,417 --> 00:35:40,119
[SPEAKER_01]: They would have exactly the same direction of how you would have started.

392
00:35:40,620 --> 00:35:45,244
[SPEAKER_01]: Let me just focus on here and now and not think about these things, because I don't know how to deal with the pressure when it rises.

393
00:35:45,744 --> 00:35:47,485
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet the sutta clearly says how to deal with it.

394
00:35:48,065 --> 00:35:49,305
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't follow it along.

395
00:35:49,325 --> 00:35:53,747
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't say concentrate away until those phenomena never come again.

396
00:35:54,487 --> 00:35:54,667
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

397
00:35:55,007 --> 00:36:00,209
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't follow along and instead think about the implicit results that are already known.

398
00:36:01,790 --> 00:36:06,271
[SPEAKER_01]: Maintain them diligently within that framework and desire will subside.

399
00:36:07,031 --> 00:36:10,953
[SPEAKER_01]: And you would have risen above the past and the future and everything else.

400
00:36:11,713 --> 00:36:14,414
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you would have been now fully disconnected from it.

401
00:36:15,798 --> 00:36:35,221
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the point to recognize, like, any time, any, any, pretty much any time you engage with the practice, whatever technique, whatever method, whatever sort of practice you're doing, but if that is rooted in dealing with phenomena that are ground for lust, if it's aiming at them,

402
00:36:38,465 --> 00:36:39,686
[SPEAKER_01]: You are not seeing the mind.

403
00:36:39,726 --> 00:36:41,607
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not seeing the basis for training the mind.

404
00:36:42,228 --> 00:36:45,190
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means the mind will not get the right message.

405
00:36:45,250 --> 00:36:48,052
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind will not penetrate through what it needs to penetrate.

406
00:36:48,412 --> 00:36:53,215
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you keep basically pointing at the wrong things as the as the root of the problem.

407
00:36:54,676 --> 00:36:57,378
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when it says the mind penetrates, means okay.

408
00:36:57,778 --> 00:37:04,823
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you would have stopped trying to deal with past, future, present phenomena, the ground for last, as the problem.

409
00:37:07,257 --> 00:37:11,360
[SPEAKER_01]: Drew a line to not give in by body, by speech.

410
00:37:12,001 --> 00:37:20,307
[SPEAKER_01]: Mentally started being diligent to not follow the choices of, oh, I'll think along them, along with what they offer, even if it's just a little bit.

411
00:37:20,347 --> 00:37:20,827
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, no.

412
00:37:21,608 --> 00:37:25,951
[SPEAKER_01]: Instead of thinking along, I'll bring up something else to be there with that pressure.

413
00:37:27,212 --> 00:37:31,255
[SPEAKER_01]: So that the mind remembers, like, oh, this is what's waiting for me when I go there.

414
00:37:32,963 --> 00:37:39,727
[SPEAKER_01]: And that, as I said, can be an immediate kind of disgust in regard to what's wanted or can be like a broad implications of where this leads to.

415
00:37:39,787 --> 00:37:42,489
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever helps the mind, sit back down.

416
00:37:43,649 --> 00:37:49,873
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever helps the dog that jumped up ready to go, sit back down and reconsider the direction he wants to go.

417
00:37:51,595 --> 00:37:54,657
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it was like that... kind of reminded me...

418
00:37:54,697 --> 00:37:57,098
[SPEAKER_01]: I think it was that Forest Masters, that book or something.

419
00:37:57,118 --> 00:37:58,079
[SPEAKER_01]: I think I mentioned it once.

420
00:37:58,659 --> 00:38:05,043
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't think I've finished it, but it was like these kind of stories about the Thai monks at the time, and I think one of the

421
00:38:06,873 --> 00:38:34,983
[SPEAKER_01]: Ajahn Chah contemporaries, or something like that, he was like living in the forest, but then his head monk called him back to go back to the temple to do his duties, and then he was stuck there for like over a year, and the mind just got infected with sensuality, fantasies, because he was just like living in a busy Bangkok place with like women around and stuff, and then he was like like really kind of on the verge of just disrobing and started fantasizing, you know, get a good Bangkok wife, start a business together, he has all these ideas, start a family,

422
00:38:36,033 --> 00:38:39,480
[SPEAKER_01]: But then he... that's obviously worked for him, and you could see.

423
00:38:40,101 --> 00:38:42,607
[SPEAKER_01]: But then he was like, oh, well, let me take that further.

424
00:38:43,616 --> 00:38:44,597
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it was like, oh, yeah, okay.

425
00:38:44,617 --> 00:38:46,357
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I could disrobe and, you know, find a wife.

426
00:38:46,437 --> 00:38:47,878
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe there are already some candidates.

427
00:38:48,458 --> 00:38:49,899
[SPEAKER_01]: Do this, do that.

428
00:38:50,699 --> 00:38:53,221
[SPEAKER_01]: Have a... buy a house, have a good income.

429
00:38:53,961 --> 00:38:57,843
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, we'll probably, you know, start arguing, start disagreeing.

430
00:38:57,923 --> 00:38:59,564
[SPEAKER_01]: She'll be like this, I'll be like that.

431
00:39:00,004 --> 00:39:00,985
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, we'll probably divorce.

432
00:39:01,225 --> 00:39:02,245
[SPEAKER_01]: They're probably this, they're probably that.

433
00:39:02,345 --> 00:39:07,368
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, his whole desire went around in a full loop and came back to, like, oh, I might as well not go down that path.

434
00:39:07,888 --> 00:39:09,569
[SPEAKER_01]: Which, kind of, bought him some time.

435
00:39:10,473 --> 00:39:16,715
[SPEAKER_01]: Until, I think, look, whatever the story is, Ajahn Mahn passed through and then took him with him, type of thing, and that kind of saved him.

436
00:39:16,755 --> 00:39:19,275
[SPEAKER_01]: But, it is already implicit.

437
00:39:19,355 --> 00:39:30,779
[SPEAKER_01]: And even if you stop, say, when there is no acute desire, you can think in those broad terms, and it does not necessarily mean it will be abstract or inaccurate, if those broad terms start pertaining to the actual implications of your desire.

438
00:39:31,019 --> 00:39:33,019
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as I said, you have to work there.

439
00:39:33,059 --> 00:39:39,841
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to kind of, not just go through the motions of a subha or something, but actually know, okay, let me philosophically consider this.

440
00:39:41,612 --> 00:39:42,573
[SPEAKER_01]: Where would this lead me?

441
00:39:42,893 --> 00:39:45,155
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, do I already not know?

442
00:39:45,235 --> 00:39:46,616
[SPEAKER_01]: Have I not tried it before?

443
00:39:46,716 --> 00:39:47,816
[SPEAKER_01]: Have I not done it before?

444
00:39:48,497 --> 00:39:50,558
[SPEAKER_01]: What are the implications of me committing to this?

445
00:39:50,618 --> 00:39:52,760
[SPEAKER_01]: And really allow those answers to sink in.

446
00:39:52,920 --> 00:39:59,705
[SPEAKER_01]: That's already a form of regathering a certain context that can help the mind from just automatically following along the lines.

447
00:40:00,885 --> 00:40:09,892
[SPEAKER_01]: So, seeing a divorce as an implicit, subsequent, inescapable outcome, separation from any type of pairing, it's already like,

448
00:40:12,021 --> 00:40:15,084
[SPEAKER_01]: It does devalue it quite a bit, if you allow it to sink in.

449
00:40:16,225 --> 00:40:21,369
[SPEAKER_01]: If you make the effort to actually reflect and think about it, and have those thoughts, those contexts, present and enduring.

450
00:40:22,209 --> 00:40:24,011
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's how the mind penetrates things.

451
00:40:24,952 --> 00:40:27,654
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, it's your own mind, the animal, that doesn't want that.

452
00:40:28,274 --> 00:40:38,383
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when those phenomena come up, that pressure, that was pretty much your own animal, untamed animal, going, immediately leaping up at the possibility, lessens.

453
00:40:39,973 --> 00:40:41,915
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the animal already sees what's implicit.

454
00:40:42,736 --> 00:40:43,737
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you've shown it enough.

455
00:40:44,057 --> 00:40:50,803
[SPEAKER_01]: You haven't been trying to replace those things blindly and deal with those phenomena or concentrate away or not experience them ever again.

456
00:40:51,324 --> 00:40:51,444
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

457
00:40:51,484 --> 00:40:54,246
[SPEAKER_01]: Instead, when they came, you broadened the context.

458
00:40:54,407 --> 00:40:56,148
[SPEAKER_01]: You really showed it what it's like.

459
00:40:57,209 --> 00:40:57,429
[SPEAKER_01]: Same.

460
00:40:57,470 --> 00:41:03,095
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you know, a child wants to, I don't know, stick its hand into a power... power... in a plug.

461
00:41:04,091 --> 00:41:09,917
[SPEAKER_01]: You can tell it, no, no, and for some children, maybe, yeah, that will be enough, but somebody, no, they won't see it.

462
00:41:09,957 --> 00:41:11,138
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you show them what happens.

463
00:41:12,660 --> 00:41:16,404
[SPEAKER_01]: You show them the implicit result of what will happen if you do it.

464
00:41:18,586 --> 00:41:26,354
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like, that's something like, you need to make these connections, not just abstractly good, but really, like, feel those connections, like, that this is a real

465
00:41:27,719 --> 00:41:29,360
[SPEAKER_01]: A good chance this would be a real outcome.

466
00:41:29,540 --> 00:41:31,941
[SPEAKER_01]: This is the implicit 90% unavoidable.

467
00:41:32,021 --> 00:41:37,303
[SPEAKER_01]: And if it's avoidable, it would be something else in terms of specificity, but still sharing pretty much the same nature.

468
00:41:38,284 --> 00:41:49,889
[SPEAKER_01]: Separation, disgust, aging, loss of senses, all of those are implicit things that are unavoidable and they are not a problem for as long as you are not dependent on the senses.

469
00:41:51,350 --> 00:41:52,712
[SPEAKER_01]: And how you're dependent on the senses?

470
00:41:52,752 --> 00:42:01,364
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, through having sense desires, unchecked, growing, proliferating, becoming your mode of being as the mode of... the only way of knowing how to escape the pain.

471
00:42:03,137 --> 00:42:04,738
[SPEAKER_01]: That's when those things become a problem.

472
00:42:04,898 --> 00:42:23,984
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you realize, okay, so, before I engage with these things, if I always have the context of the implicit outcome of my own senses, for example, which is kind of immediate and delayed at the same time, the implication can be recognized on both levels, that's already enough to, like, oh, a sense of danger of going down this path.

473
00:42:25,325 --> 00:42:31,507
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you can refine it further and further to any extent, to match any extent the desire or pressure arises.

474
00:42:32,488 --> 00:42:35,250
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's a lot, coarse, give it a broad one.

475
00:42:35,650 --> 00:42:38,232
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's these subtle pushes, well, match them to that level.

476
00:42:38,932 --> 00:42:41,014
[SPEAKER_01]: But the point is, the mind needs to understand it.

477
00:42:41,094 --> 00:42:43,375
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind needs to penetrate it through and through.

478
00:42:45,577 --> 00:42:57,865
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's not going to happen through the mere practice of just think about something else, don't think about the future, just stop thinking, stop thinking, concentrate now, the techniques and stuff, that's just like completely, as I said, obscures it even further.

479
00:42:59,795 --> 00:43:08,524
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're reinforcing the view that the problem was in those phenomena, not in your own desire and untrained mind that remains like even... like, well, it becomes even worse now.

480
00:43:08,604 --> 00:43:09,585
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you're not even seeing it.

481
00:43:10,666 --> 00:43:22,778
[SPEAKER_01]: And any chance to see it now has been buried even deeper through engaging in developing further these wrong views that become further and further fetters and increasing of the obstacles.

482
00:43:23,259 --> 00:43:23,719
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like...

483
00:43:27,857 --> 00:43:30,098
[SPEAKER_01]: Somebody did send that.

484
00:43:30,198 --> 00:43:41,224
[SPEAKER_01]: It's quite a good sutta, just that adds to this whole kind of practice we described, with both suttas together, really.

485
00:43:42,345 --> 00:43:51,330
[SPEAKER_01]: Recognition that sense desires are an issue, and even if, say, I don't want to be a monk, I don't want to train for enlightenment,

486
00:43:53,955 --> 00:43:58,899
[SPEAKER_01]: That still doesn't mean you should just completely keep your desires unchecked.

487
00:43:59,620 --> 00:44:05,324
[SPEAKER_01]: It will still benefit you, even in this life, let alone, like, you know, the subsequent lives and so on.

488
00:44:05,644 --> 00:44:18,915
[SPEAKER_01]: But the point is, yeah, so, when people hear that, the sensuality, the problem of it, say... say they're not necessarily finding faults, by default,

489
00:44:19,970 --> 00:44:24,094
[SPEAKER_01]: They're not necessarily saying, oh, no, this is not a problem, like, like these, you know, the three types of people.

490
00:44:24,874 --> 00:44:28,918
[SPEAKER_01]: Which, by the way, it wasn't like... it was common back in the Buddha's day as well.

491
00:44:29,358 --> 00:44:39,146
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what this person said in the Sutta, saying, like, yeah, when the Majjhima 75, when... when he called the Buddha the... he's the destroyer of growth.

492
00:44:39,827 --> 00:44:41,308
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he was trying to disparage the Buddha.

493
00:44:41,869 --> 00:44:46,112
[SPEAKER_01]: By disparaging the Buddha, you then disparage everything that he tells you you need to do for liberation.

494
00:44:47,093 --> 00:44:49,835
[SPEAKER_01]: And he says, well, what do you mean by destroyer of growth?

495
00:44:49,856 --> 00:45:00,985
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you mean that the I that delights in forms, takes delight in forms, rejoices in forms, has been tamed by Tathāgata, has been subdued, guarded, protected, restrained?

496
00:45:02,585 --> 00:45:06,986
[SPEAKER_01]: The dhāgata teaches the dhamma for the purpose of restraint of the senses.

497
00:45:07,566 --> 00:45:12,408
[SPEAKER_01]: Is the reference to that, what you said, that Recluse Gautama is destroyer of growth?

498
00:45:12,808 --> 00:45:18,850
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said, yeah, yeah, that's that's why we call you destroyer of growth, because you're telling us to practice sense restraint, basically.

499
00:45:18,870 --> 00:45:19,870
[SPEAKER_00]: You're a destroyer of growth.

500
00:45:19,890 --> 00:45:22,270
[SPEAKER_01]: You're a destroyer of the of the nature, of the right things, or whatever.

501
00:45:22,571 --> 00:45:27,692
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, just trying to disparage him, so to not have to do it.

502
00:45:31,472 --> 00:45:41,454
